{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Swears falsely concerning it and pays.</b> We have learned the punishment; from where the warning? The Talmud says: לא תשקרו." "<b>And he shall pay it in its principal.</b> He pays its principal and does not pay double payment, nor fourfold and fivefold. Another interpretation: <b>and he shall pay it in its principal</b> — before bringing his offering, he must remove the stolen property from his possession." "<b>And he shall add a fifth to it.</b> Two fifths: its fifth and a fifth of the fifth. And when does he pay two fifths? When he does not confess but [is convicted] by the testimony of witnesses. But if he confesses on his own, he adds only one fifth." "<b>He shall give it on the day of his guilt-offering.</b> The robber shall give the money to the victim on the day he brings his guilt-offering, to turn back from his sin, so that his offering may be accepted favorably." "<b>From all that he swears upon</b> etc. To bring the sliding-scale offering, because it is not mentioned in the verse above regarding the oath of testimony, and one who enters the sanctuary impure, and one who eats consecrated food in impurity, and an oath of utterance — since they do not benefit from the transgression, the All-Merciful was lenient upon them to bring an offering that goes up and down. This means: if he goes up in wealth he brings a ewe or a she-goat, and if he goes down in assets he brings two turtledoves or two young pigeons or a tenth of an ephah. [But] one who eats fat and blood, and one who eats on Yom HaKippurim, and one who does labor on the Sabbath, and one who compounds the anointing oil, and one who anoints [with it], and one who lies with a close relative — who benefit — and all the more so one who serves idolatry, who stretches out his hand against the very essence, are obligated to bring a fixed sin-offering. Those who benefit from consecrated things, since there are two [aspects] — he stretched out his hand against what belongs to the Holy One, blessed be He, and benefited — are obligated [to bring] a guilt-offering valued at two shekels. And likewise one who denies his fellow regarding a deposit, since there are two [aspects] — he benefited and swore falsely — is obligated [to bring] a guilt-offering valued at two shekels. Those obligated for a suspended guilt-offering — because they act leniently and rule permit for themselves to bring themselves into doubt, the verse was stringent upon them to bring a guilt-offering valued at two shekels, so that they will be careful with themselves."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:10:15.132504676Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}